Falsifies `generational_table::verification::live_names_are_distinct_and_resolve_to_their_own_entry`.

The defect: `EntryName::new` packs the two fields the other way round while `unpack` is left
alone. The pair has to agree and nothing makes them, which is what this kind of hand-rolled
packing costs; the crate doc says "a name packs `(generation, slot)`" and reading that order into
the shift is the error.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(t.get(b).copied(), Some(b_val))`. The first slot's
name survives the transposition (both halves are zero), so a table that is only ever used one
entry at a time would look correct; the second entry's name unpacks to slot 0 with generation 1
and resolves to nothing.
diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
index 09b6084..1286e1f 100644
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -127,7 +127,7 @@ impl<T> EntryName<T> {
 
     /// The name of `slot` at `generation`: the generation in the high 32 bits, the slot in the low.
     const fn new(slot: usize, generation: u32) -> Self {
-        Self::from_bits(((generation as u64) << 32) | slot as u64)
+        Self::from_bits(((slot as u64) << 32) | generation as u64)
     }
 }
 
